menu "Hypervisor"

config HYPERVISOR
	bool "Launch a tiny type-1 hypervisor for debugging"
	depends on ARM64
	help
	  When U-Boot is launched in EL2, install a small bare
	  metal hypervisor which can optionally take over a USB
	  port and allow U-Boot or the kernel to be debugged.

	  Inspired by (and aiming to be compatible with) m1n1.

config HYP_USB
	bool "Take the USB controller over from the guest"
	depends on HYPERVISOR && ARCH_SNAPDRAGON
	default y
	select OF_BOARD_SETUP
	select OF_LIBFDT_OVERLAY
	help
	  Take the board's USB controller away from the EL1 guest once it
	  has finished with it, for the hypervisor's own debug link
	  (scripts/USB_GADGET.md).  The controller is the DT node tagged
	  `hypervisor-takeover`.  The guest's board setup releases its own
	  drivers, deletes the node from the device tree it hands to the
	  OS and issues the HV_USB_TAKEOVER hypercall; from then on a guest
	  access to the controller's registers reads zero.

config HYP_USB_HALT
	bool "Halt the guest after the USB takeover until `boot`"
	depends on HYP_USB
	# default y
	help
	  Once the USB debug link is up, stop the guest inside its
	  HV_USB_TAKEOVER hypercall and wait for `boot` on the hypervisor's
	  telnet console (192.168.77.2:2323) before the OS is started, so
	  that a GDB session or a console logger can be attached first.
	  Without it the OS starts as soon as the link is up.

config HYP_SPY_RPMH
	bool "Demo MMIO spy on the RPMH resource state coordinator"
	depends on HYPERVISOR
	help
	  Register the apps_rsc (RPMH-RSC) register region with the
	  stage-2 MMIO watch (hyp_mmio_watch) from the RPMH hyp driver
	  probe, and decode the guest's TCS programming - named
	  regulator votes and all - to the console.  See
	  drivers/soc/qcom/rpmh-rsc.c and scripts/RPMH_SPY.md.

config HYP_RPMH_BCM_CAP
	bool "Cap the guest's saturated interconnect bandwidth votes"
	depends on HYPERVISOR
	default y
	help
	  Until sync_state the OS's interconnect driver votes every
	  bandwidth BCM at its saturated maximum.  The vote on the memory
	  controller makes the AOP raise DDR to its top frequency, which
	  resets the board.  Watch the apps_rsc window and clamp such
	  votes to HYP_RPMH_BCM_CAP_VOTE.  See drivers/soc/qcom/rpmh-rsc.c.

config HYP_RPMH_BCM_CAP_VOTE
	hex "Largest bandwidth vote (x and y, 14 bits) the OS may send"
	depends on HYP_RPMH_BCM_CAP
	default 0x400

config HYP_TEST
	bool "Run the hypervisor self-test suite on every boot"
	depends on HYPERVISOR
	help
	  Build hyp/test.c and run its cases from hv_start_vm(), at EL2,
	  before the guest is handed control.  Every case asserts on
	  registers, structs, return values and memory - never on console
	  wording - and the run ends with a [HYPTEST] SUMMARY line; any
	  failure halts the board.  Off for normal builds; scripts/test/run.sh
	  adds it through configs/hyp-test.config.  See scripts/TESTING.md.
endmenu
